/** \page parameters Parameters

The tables below list the names, default values, data types (patterns), descriptions, and
aliases that can be read by PRISMS-PF's `UserInputParameters`. The tables are organized by
subsection, with the first table listing the "top-level" parameters.

Entries in the parameters file are either top-level or contained in subsections. The first
table below gives the top-level parameters. The remaining tables give the parameters
inside each type of subsection. For top-level entries, the format is:
@code{}
set Parameter Name = value
@endcode
Substitute `Parameter Name` with one of the names listed in the tables below, and
substitute `value` with the desired value for the parameter. The expected data type for
`value` is given in the tables below for each parameter.

For subsections, the format is:
@code{}
subsection Section Name <index>
  set Parameter Name = value
  set Parameter Name = value
  ...
end
@endcode
Not all subsections require an `<index>`. Some subsections can be repeated multiple times,
in which case `<index>` should be given as an integer value starting from 0 and counting
up. For example, the subsection `boundary conditions:` can be repeated multiple times, to
allow different fields to be assigned different sets of boundary conditions. In that case,
you may have:
@code{}
subsection boundary conditions: 0
  set variables = n1, n2
  set conditions = NATURAL, NATURAL, NATURAL, NATURAL
end
subsection boundary conditions: 1
  set variables = n3, n4
  set conditions = DIRICHLET, DIRICHLET, NATURAL, NATURAL
end
@endcode
By default, the parameter handler can read a maximum of `Numbers::default_subsections`
subsections of each type (this default value is 10 for PRISMS-PF 4.0). If you want to
include even more copies of a given subsection (e.g., you need to specify 40 different
boundary conditions), then you can increase the maximum subsection count when reading the
parameters file in your code (typically in `main.cc`):
@code{.cpp}
UserInputParameters<dim>(cli_options.get_parameters_filename(), 40);
@endcode

Not all parameters need to be specified in your parameters file. Any parameter that is not
specified will have a default value as given in the tables below. For examples, see the
`parameters.prm` files contained in each example application.

Comments can be added to the parameters file; lines starting with `#` are comments.
Additionally, all the parameters have aliases for a handful of common case styles (e.g.,
PascalCase, snake_case, Upper Case, and Pascal_Snake_Case).

@note You can copy parameter names to clipboard by clicking on them!

@parameter_list

*/
